# What no command here can answer, and the panel that answers it.
#
# `judges  text`, comma separated, and each judge is one word. `.foundry/gates` holds the half a
# command can settle. This holds the half it cannot.
#
# **A `reach` line says how the runner asks one of them.** `reach  judges  command...`, and the
# command is last so spaces and quotes need no parser. Without one a clause still derives and only a
# person can answer it; with one, `run.sh judged` runs the command and reads what came back.
#
# **`reach` is a reserved first word**, so no judge may be called it. Two record kinds in one file
# need a word telling them apart, and only the first field can carry one — the text is already the
# line's tail, and a line holds one tail.
#
# **A member is a model at a bench**, written `<model>:<role>`. The role is the part played, and
# Panel ships the parts. The model is who stands there.
#
# **Every member must approve, and one dissent stops the work.** A majority would let the members who
# looked hardest be outvoted by the ones who did not.
#
# A repository may name whoever it trusts. `panel:adversary` on one model is a real check: a
# different role reads for different faults, and finds them. What it cannot claim is a second
# mind, because it runs in the producing session on the same model.
#
# Foundry takes the stricter bar for itself, because what it judges here is its own runtime. That is
# this repository's choice and not a rule it ships.
#
# One member today, so this is a panel of one. A second model is the next piece of work.
#
# **The reach below names an adapter floor ships, and this repository owns no line of it.** The
# digest is what says which content may judge here. Upgrading is editing that word, in a commit this
# history keeps — never something that happens to a machine.
#
# Take a new one with `git hash-object --no-filters -- plugins/floor/adapters/codex-exec/run.sh`.

